{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_012YKTAfXmh7jVnGL8ZdBQEU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Wrote **`answers/01-021-how-do-you-do-memory-management-and-context-management-with.md`** — Q21: How do you do memory management and context management with LLMs?\n\n**Key content:**\n- **Four memory tiers:** in-context sliding window (working memory) → external vector store (Pinecone/pgvector for semantic recall) → summarization layer (async cheap-model call, LangChain ConversationSummaryBufferMemory) → structured DB/Redis (for exact-match facts like prices/IDs)\n- **Why each tier exists:** cost/TTFT pressure from long context; lost-in-the-middle degradation; unbounded history that vector search handles elegantly; correctness failures when vector search is used for exact facts\n- **Prompt compression:** LLMLingua for 2–4× chunk compression before injection\n- **Production example:** 40% token reduction, p95 latency 4.2s → 2.1s on a 1M conv/day chatbot\n- **Pitfalls covered:** naive full-history pass-through, using vector store for exact-recall facts, no eviction policy\n\nUp next: **Q22 — What is self-attention? How does it differ from multi-head attention?**"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":468,"cache_read_input_tokens":56329,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":468,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ac80cf6-37ae-42ec-a9b1-ecff046a895a","uuid":"d8028651-4d68-4e31-8ec1-032781f8de9b"}
